  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 220 PM Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A cold front sliding south across central North \nCarolina will bring the potential for heavy rain, particularly to \nthe south and east of the Triangle, along with much more seasonable \ntemperatures.\n\nThrough the day, a cold front has swept south across much of North \nCarolina. In counties along the Virginia/North Carolina border, 2pm \ntemperatures were only in the 60s, with 70s along and to the south \nof US-64, and 80s contained to the warm side of the front across the \nsouthern part of the state. A line of rain currently extends along \nmuch of the US-64 corridor, although there is a gap near the Triad. \nWhile this line has been the focus of rain during the late morning \nand early afternoon hours, high-resolution models suggest that the \ncharacter of the rain will become more of a cluster late this \nafternoon into the evening hours, primarily south and east of the \nTriangle, as a wave of low pressure forms along the front. South of \nUS-64, most locations will have at least a quarter inch of rain this \nafternoon, with 0.5 to 1 inch of rain east of Raleigh and \nFayetteville. There may be a bit of a lull this evening, with \nrainfall ramping up once again after midnight. While locations to \nthe north of US-64 should generally have less than a half inch of \nrain through sunrise Sunday, most locations south of US-64 will have \nat least three quarters of an inch of rain, with some locations \nreceiving 2 inches of rain. Isolated locations with excessive \nrainfall cannot be ruled out. Overnight lows will range from the mid \n50s across the north to the mid 60s across the south.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Periods of light rain and cool temperatures remain \npossible through the day on Sunday before a reinforcing cold front \nmoves through the region Monday.\n\nToday's cold front will stall to our south tonight, before \npotentially wavering back north over the region in the south \ntomorrow. At the same time, cool high pressure will continue to \nextend southward into central NC through the day on Sunday, allowing \nfor a cool wedge airmass to remain in place. Thus, cloudy skies with \nperiods of light to moderate rain remain possible, especially in the \nsouth and east closer to the frontal boundary and the coast. \nHowever, CAM guidance is still trending drier in regards to the \nrainfall amounts possible, especially in the Triad region. Many \nmodels suggest that the Triad may remain dry, or only receive \nminimal rain on Sunday, while the 12Z HREF suggests that the eastern \nhalf of the CWA may only receive up to 0.5 inch of rain. However, \nlooking at the LPMM data, it will be possible for a few locations in \nthe east to receive up to an inch of rain. \n\nA reinforcing cold front will move through the region on Monday. \nThis should eliminate any rain chances for the rest of the week as \ncool and dry high pressure builds back in. Monday may warm up ahead \nof the frontal passage into the mid to perhaps upper 70s, with \nTuesday and Wednesday cooling into the upper 60s to low/mid 70s, \nwith Tuesday looking to be the coolest day. Lows look to drop into \nthe 40s Tuesday night as well. Temperatures will then warm back to \nslightly above normal to end the week ahead of the next frontal \npassage next weekend.",
